\subsection{Numerical questions and the objects being compared}
\label{num:scope}
The numerical study follows the two control objectives derived in
Section~\ref{sec:derivation}. Route A restores the nominal, coupled
Hamiltonian $h_0$. Route B retains the fitted Hamiltonian $\widehat h$ as
a predictive model and tracks one of its conservative trajectories.
Both routes act on the same synthetic damped plant and use the same
identified coefficients. Their reference motions differ, so their error
levels answer different questions and are reported separately.

The new calculations isolate a controlled, single-feature potential
perturbation. For the scalar function $g$ of
Section~\ref{sec:derivation}, define
\begin{equation}
 \begin{split}
 \phi(X)&=\nabla_X g(d^{\mathsf T}X)\\
        &=d[\sin(d^{\mathsf T}X)-d^{\mathsf T}X].
 \end{split}
 \label{num:feature-gradient}
\end{equation}
The true and fitted perturbations are
\begin{equation}
 \begin{split}
 R_*(X)&=\beta_*g(d^{\mathsf T}X),\qquad \beta_*=0.2,\\
 \widehat R(X)&=\widehat\beta g(d^{\mathsf T}X).
 \end{split}
 \label{num:feature}
\end{equation}
The second fitted scalar is the dimensionless drag $\gamma$.
The coefficient $0.2$ defines a synthetic stress test; it is not an
identified material discrepancy. This two-parameter experiment verifies
the passage from state increments to a conservative residual, damping
compensation and physical input reconstruction. A general CHNN may have
many coefficients and momentum-dependent terms. Its approximation,
optimization and generalization errors are not measured by this test.

Five error sources remain distinct: uncertainty in physical inputs,
spatial and modal discretization, coefficient identification, time
integration, and feedback tracking. The supplied records identify the
source of each reported quantity. In particular, mesh convergence does
not establish a two-mode truncation error, a small regression residual
does not establish a uniform analytic error, and a small error against
a jointly discretized reference does not by itself resolve the error
against the corresponding continuous trajectory. The experiments below
use separate checks for these interfaces.

\subsection{Recomputed finite-element coordinates and parameter sensitivity}
\label{num:fe}
All modal quantities are recomputed from the material and magnet choices
in Section~\ref{sec:derivation}. The material reference values are
$E\simeq2.1$ MPa and $\rho\simeq1028$ kg/m$^3$, with the formulation and
curing conditions reported by \citet{cho2021pdms}. The free length
$80$ mm and solid radius $0.6$ mm are research design inputs. A nominal
$0.250$ mm cubic magnetic core, $B_r=1.425$ T and density
$7500$ kg/m$^3$ give the engineering estimate
$\mu\simeq@@MU@@$ A\,m$^2$, together with
$m_t=@@TIP_MASS@@$ kg and
$J_t=@@TIP_J@@$ kg\,m$^2$.
The remanence and density are interpreted as manufacturer-grade
information and a selected nominal point, respectively
\citep{smN50,kjMoment}; they are not a calibration of an assembled device.

The translational mass and rotary inertia of the core enter the mass
matrix before modal reduction. The bias contribution enters the
stiffness through $\mu B_0ss^{\mathsf T}$, where $s$ reads the endpoint
rotation. Consistent cubic-Hermite assemblies with $16$, $32$ and $64$
elements are compared with a separate continuum boundary determinant
for this same ideal endpoint model. At $32$ elements the largest
relative error among the first four frequencies is $@@FE32_MAX@@$;
at $64$ elements it is $@@FE64_MAX@@$.
Figure~\ref{num:verification}(a) and
Table~\ref{sx:fe-table} show the complete comparison.

The $32$-element basis supplies
\begin{align}
 \omega_0=\omega_1&=@@OMEGA0@@\ \mathrm{rad/s},\notag\\
 \omega_2&=@@OMEGA2@@\ \mathrm{rad/s},\qquad
 \nu_2=@@NU2@@,\notag\\
 d&=@@D_VECTOR@@,\notag\\
 \ell&=@@ELL_VECTOR@@\ \mathrm m.
 \label{num:scales}
\end{align}
The same $d$, $\ell$, frequencies and energy scale are used in the
data generator, fitted model, controls and physical readouts. Relative
to the biased beam with no endpoint inertia, including the core changes
the first frequency by $@@TIP_EFFECT_PERCENT@@\%$. Even this modest
change requires a consistent update of the modal maps.

The baseline damping convention is explicitly
$C=\alpha M_{\mathrm{total}}$, with
$\zeta_1^{\mathrm{d}}=0.025$ at the biased first frequency, hence
$\alpha=@@ALPHA@@$ s$^{-1}$ and $\gamma=0.05$.
It gives $\zeta_2^{\mathrm{d}}=@@ZETA2@@$, rather than equal damping ratios for
both modes. Separate design perturbations vary $E$ by $20\%$, core
inertia by $10\%$, and remanence over the stated grade interval.
Table~\ref{sx:sensitivity-table} reports their frequency consequences
while holding the nominal $\alpha$ fixed. These are sensitivity points,
not material confidence intervals. The below-nominal inertia point is
an independent matrix perturbation and does not assert a below-specification
density for a fixed-size N50 core.

\subsection{Shared noiseless integral identification}
\label{num:identification}
Four training trajectories and two disjoint validation trajectories are
generated from the same two-mode plant. Each lasts $\tau=4$ and is
sampled every $0.002$ dimensionless time units. Composite Simpson
integration on windows of length $0.04$ produces $400$ training
vector increments, or $800$ scalar rows. The initial states are fixed
and listed in Table~\ref{sx:initial-states}; no test state enters the
least-squares fit. Both routes regenerate these observations and
recover identical coefficients before any control comparison.

For a window $W=[\tau_a,\tau_b]$, the fitted balance is
\begin{equation}
 \begin{split}
 b_W&=Y_b-Y_a+\int_W\{\mathsf NX+\phi(X)\}\,\mathrm d\tau,\\
 A_W&=\left[-\int_W\phi(X)\,\mathrm d\tau,
                 -\int_WY\,\mathrm d\tau\right],\\
 A_W\binom{\beta}{\gamma}&\simeq b_W.
 \end{split}
 \label{num:regression}
\end{equation}
Here $\mathsf N=\operatorname{diag}(\nu_1,\nu_2)$. Columns are scaled before
unconstrained least squares and the coefficients are then returned
to the original units. No regularization, derivative labels or
measurement noise are introduced. The recovered values are
\begin{equation}
 \widehat\beta=@@BETA_HAT@@,\qquad
 \widehat\gamma=@@GAMMA_HAT@@.
 \label{num:fitted-values}
\end{equation}
Training and held-out scalar-increment RMS residuals are
$@@TRAIN_RMSE@@$ and $@@VALID_RMSE@@$, respectively.
They are nonzero because the states and integrals are computed
numerically, despite the absence of observational noise.

The raw design condition number is $@@COND_RAW@@$, whereas column
scaling reduces it to $@@COND_SCALED@@$. This improves the numerical
solve without increasing the physical excitation of the weak cubic
feature. The normalized Gram matrix $G=A^{\mathsf T}A/800$ has
$\lambda_{\min}(G)=@@GRAM_MIN@@$. Consequently the coefficient
bound discussed in Section~\ref{sec:kam} amplifies a residual or
integration-defect bound by $@@COEFF_FACTOR@@$.
The small observed residual must therefore be read together with
this scale and the declared feature family. The known synthetic
truth permits a diagnostic coefficient error of $@@COEFF_ACTUAL@@$;
it does not provide an independent rigorous integration-defect bound.
The experiment establishes in-family recovery under its stated
observations, rather than statistical learning performance or a new
learning-to-KAM certificate.

\subsection{Time discretization and independent reference checks}
\label{num:integration}
The principal comparisons run to $\tau=20$, corresponding to
$@@TIME_S@@$ s, with midpoint step $\Delta=0.001$.
Newton iteration uses the full midpoint Jacobian, an increment-residual
infinity-norm tolerance $2\times10^{-14}$ and at most $12$ correction
attempts. Failure to converge stops the run. These tolerances describe
the algebraic solve and are separate from the time-discretization error.

An explicit RK4 calculation supplies each independent conservative
reference, using steps $0.0005$ and $0.00025$ for refinement.
The maximum common-time differences are $@@A_REFINE@@$ for $h_0$
and $@@B_REFINE@@$ for $\widehat h$.
These numerical comparisons resolve the much larger midpoint errors
reported here; they are not interval enclosures of the exact flow.
For the route-A oracle, midpoint step halvings from $0.04$ to $0.005$
give orders approaching two, with the final estimate $@@ORDER_LAST@@$
(Table~\ref{sx:order-table}). The complete-cosine target is retained
throughout. The fourth-order central construction in
Section~\ref{sec:derivation} has a separate theoretical role;
no earlier Central4 error table is reused as a new-baseline experiment.

The oracle route-A closed field is Hamiltonian. At the solved
midpoints its ideal Cayley tangent has maximum symplectic defect
$@@A_CAYLEY@@$. This checks the tangent of the implicitly defined
map at numerical solution points. It does not imply exact
symplecticity of floating-point Newton code. With fitted cancellation,
the remaining damping coefficient is nonzero, however small, so a
strict canonical-symplectic conclusion is not assigned to that loop.
Route-B tracking has a dissipative error dynamics and receives no
symplecticity claim. This separates the analytical structure statements
from the finite-precision diagnostics used to assess their implementation.

\subsection{Route A: restoration of the nominal conservative dynamics}
\label{num:route-a}
The route-A comparison starts the plant and the $h_0$ reference at
$(X_1,X_2,Y_1,Y_2)=(0.09,0.08,0.015,-0.01)$.
Passive evolution, fitted damping compensation, fitted full restoration
and an oracle using $(\beta_*,\gamma_*)$ are tested on this same initial
value problem. Every nonzero generalized command passes through the
magnetic-port reconstruction of Section~\ref{sec:application} before
it is applied to the plant. The oracle is an assessment benchmark;
the learned controller does not read the true coefficients.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Route A at $\Delta=0.001$, $0\leq\tau\leq20$.
Both errors use an independent RK4 trajectory of $h_0$.}
\label{num:a-table}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Control & State RMS & Tip RMS ($\mu$m)\\
\midrule
@@A_ROWS@@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{num:a-table} shows a substantial reduction from the passive
state RMS $@@A_PASSIVE_RMS@@$ to $@@A_DAMP_RMS@@$ with damping
compensation. Restoring the fitted residual further reduces it to
$@@A_LEARNED_RMS@@$. The learned tip RMS is $@@A_TIP_UM@@\,\mu$m
in this idealized two-mode experiment.
Its continuous-vector-field mismatch to the target, sampled along
the controlled states, is only $@@A_VF@@$ RMS. The trajectory error
nevertheless remains close to the oracle value $@@A_ORACLE_RMS@@$
because both use the same midpoint approximation to an independent
continuous reference. A slightly smaller fitted-controller error is
consistent with cancellation between its small model error and the
shared phase error; it does not demonstrate improvement over exact
parameter knowledge. Route A tests restoration from a shared initial
state, not attraction from a tracking offset.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/new_baseline_results.pdf}
\caption{New-baseline comparisons using independent conservative
references. (a) Route A restores $h_0$ from the same initial state.
(b) Route B tracks a frozen $\widehat h$ trajectory from a nonzero
initial offset. Whole-window and late-window errors expose the tracking
transient. The panels compare different reference objects; their bars
are not a ranking of one route against the other.}
\label{num:results}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Route B: prediction, tracking and reference synchronization}
\label{num:route-b}
Before control is applied, conservative prediction is tested against
$h_*$ using separate fine-step RK4 trajectories from the same initial
state. The nominal $h_0$ state RMS is $@@B_PRED_NOMINAL@@$; replacing
it by the fitted $\widehat h$ gives $@@B_PRED_LEARNED@@$.
This comparison tests recovery of the chosen conservative perturbation.
The control experiment then freezes $\widehat h$, initializes its
reference at the route-A initial state, and offsets the actual plant
by $(0.0045,0.004,0,0)$. Gains $\kappa_p=1$ and $\kappa_d=2$ produce the
critical-damping oracle position-error equation derived in
Section~\ref{sec:derivation}. The same frozen reference is supplied
to all four control groups, including the oracle.

The actual and reference states are integrated as one eight-dimensional
midpoint system. A second, independently integrated RK4 reference is
retained solely for assessment. Let $x_k$ be the actual state,
$\bar x_k$ the synchronous midpoint reference and
$x_{\mathrm{ref}}(\tau_k)$ the independent reference. Then
\begin{equation}
 x_k-x_{\mathrm{ref}}(\tau_k)
 =(x_k-\bar x_k)+
   (\bar x_k-x_{\mathrm{ref}}(\tau_k)).
 \label{num:reference-split}
\end{equation}
The first term measures synchronization to the implemented reference;
the second retains its integration error. The two are reported
together in Table~\ref{num:b-table}.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\caption{Route-B state RMS at $\Delta=0.001$. ``Independent'' refers
to fine-step RK4 for the same frozen $\widehat h$; ``synchronous''
refers to the reference component of the augmented midpoint system.}
\label{num:b-table}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Control & Independent, $[0,20]$ & Independent, $[10,20]$ & Synchronous, $[10,20]$\\
\midrule
@@B_ROWS@@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

The learned controller has whole-window independent-reference RMS
$@@B_FULL_RMS@@$, including the prescribed startup error. On
$10\leq\tau\leq20$ it decreases to $@@B_HALF_RMS@@$, while the
synchronous RMS is $@@B_HALF_SYNC@@$.
At the endpoint, the synchronous error is $@@B_FINAL_SYNC@@$ but
the independent-reference error is $@@B_FINAL_IND@@$.
The maximum midpoint-reference error is $@@B_REF_PHASE@@$; halving
the midpoint step reduces that error by a factor $@@B_HALF_RATIO@@$.
Thus near-perfect synchronization is compatible with a resolved
second-order reference phase error. It must not be reported as the
accuracy of the continuous reference motion.

An additional check compares the oracle tracking error against the
Cayley update of its linear error equation. The maximum discrepancy
is $@@B_CAYLEY@@$. This verifies the augmented midpoint coupling
without making the stable tracking loop Hamiltonian. The experiment
also shows why damping compensation alone is insufficient for this
objective: it does not remove the imposed initial position offset.

\subsection{Damping scenarios with an independent fit in every case}
\label{num:damping}
The declared first-mode damping ratios are
$\zeta_1^{\mathrm{d}}\in\{0,0.01,0.025,0.05,0.1\}$.
For each value, all four training and two validation trajectories are
regenerated and $(\widehat\beta,\widehat\gamma)$ is refitted.
The true model uses $\alpha=2\zeta_1^{\mathrm{d}}\omega_0$ and
$\gamma=2\zeta_1^{\mathrm{d}}$, so the second modal ratio is
$\zeta_1^{\mathrm{d}}/\nu_2$. The scan runs to $\tau=10$ with midpoint step
$0.002$ and an independent RK4 reference of step $0.00025$.
It therefore has a different time window and discretization floor
from the principal comparisons. The late interval for route B is
$5\leq\tau\leq10$.

Figure~\ref{num:verification}(c) and
Tables~\ref{sx:damping-fit}--\ref{sx:damping-errors} show that
the fitted and oracle controls remain close throughout this
in-family, exact-observation sweep. Their nearly unchanged errors
reflect successful cancellation together with common discretization
and startup effects. Every setting receives its own fit; this is not
a test of one frozen controller against unknown changing damping.
The zero-damping least-squares fit returns
$\widehat\gamma=@@SCAN_ZERO_GAMMA@@$. This minute negative value
comes from an unconstrained numerical solve and is not evidence of
negative material damping. The selected ratios are design scenarios;
material loss data, which depend on preparation and frequency,
cannot identify these modal ratios without an additional model or
device measurement \citep{fischer2017adhesion}.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/new_baseline_verification.pdf}
\caption{Independent numerical checks from the supplied JSON records.
(a) The first four FE frequencies converge to roots of the continuum
endpoint determinant. (b) Route-A oracle errors converge at second
order against an independent RK4 reference. (c) Each damping setting
is refitted before either route is evaluated; A uses the full $[0,10]$
window and B the late $[5,10]$ window. Lines connect tested settings
and do not represent additional simulations or statistical intervals.}
\label{num:verification}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Magnetic demands, sampled domains and the scope of the evidence}
\label{num:ports}
The modal force/torque matrix has column-normalized condition number
$@@PORT_COND@@$. Its columns have different physical units, so this
normalization is stated rather than treating a raw condition number as
a unit-independent controllability measure. Commands are reconstructed
at all stored endpoints and integration midpoints. Across the principal
tests and damping scan, the maximum generalized-command reconstruction
error is $@@ALL_PORT_ERR@@$. This verifies the local algebraic port map;
a coil-current allocation, bandwidth model and actuator calibration
are outside the present experiment.

The baseline learned demands are listed in Table~\ref{sx:port-table}.
The total local field includes the $30$ mT longitudinal bias:
$|B|=\sqrt{B_0^2+B_y^2}$. Across all runs its sampled maximum is
$@@ALL_TOTAL_MT@@$ mT, and the maximum local gradient spectral norm is
$@@ALL_GRAD@@$ T/m. Published magnetic systems provide a useful
field-scale comparison \citep{lussi2021cvs}, but they do not verify
simultaneous field/gradient feasibility for these commands.
The largest sampled endpoint angle is $@@ALL_ANGLE@@$ rad; the
largest beam slope on the $257$-point spatial grid is $@@ALL_SLOPE@@$.
The minimum sampled $|\cos\theta|$ is $@@ALL_COS@@$.
These diagnostics support the sampled small-slope operating regime
and separation from the port singularity. They are not continuous-time
or spatially certified extrema.

No saturation or energy governor is executed. For the separate
$4$ mm clearance comparison, the nominal energy threshold is
$E_c=@@EC_NJ@@$ nJ and $0.95E_c=@@BUDGET_NJ@@$ nJ, whereas the
route-A initial nominal energy is $@@INITIAL_ENERGY_NJ@@$ nJ.
Consequently the present trajectories do not satisfy the proposed
energy-cap premise at initialization, even when their sampled
displacements remain below the clearance. Section~\ref{sec:application}
states the applicable conditional energy argument.

The results resolve a minimal numerical chain on the newly sourced
benchmark. They do not carry forward the earlier CHNN contrasts,
COMSOL reduction comparisons, long-time simulations or finite-amplitude
KAM certificates. Reestablishing those conclusions requires their
respective data, fitted functions, higher-mode comparisons and
validated analytic calculations on this same new baseline.
Supplementary Section~\ref{sec:supp-experiments} records the complete
protocol and the scope of that migration.
